  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development — Develop Capabilities: Digital Certificates", "technique": "T1587.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-02-24 02:11:34 UTC, Suricata logged a TLS handshake originating from internal host 10.108.57.177 in which the presented X.509 certificate had data.tls.subject equal to data.tls.issuerdn — the textbook signature of a SELF-SIGNED certificate. The certificate Common Name was 'corp-internal-tools' with organisation 'MicrosoftUpdate' — an attacker-crafted CN designed to masquerade as a legitimate internal/Microsoft asset. Suricata raised 'SURICATA TLS invalid certificate' / self-signed anomaly events for this flow.", "chain_reason": "Suricata TLS event with data.srcip=10.108.57.177 where data.tls.subject == data.tls.issuerdn AND the certificate CN ('corp-internal-tools') does not correspond to any known PKI-issued corporate asset. Self-signed + masquerading CN is the canonical Develop-Capabilities: Digital-Certificates indicator (T1587.003).", "first_seen": "2026-02-24T02:11:34+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Resource Development — Capability Staging / Listener", "technique": "T1587.003", "what_happened": "At 02:14:09 UTC (~2.5 min later) the same host 10.108.57.177 established a loopback TLS session to 127.0.0.1 on non-standard port 44399, presenting the SAME self-signed certificate (identical data.tls.fingerprint). This is a local TLS listener / staging step — the attacker stands up and validates the C2 transport on the box itself before pointing it at external infrastructure.", "chain_reason": "Suricata TLS event data.srcip=10.108.57.177 data.dstip=127.0.0.1 data.dstport=44399 carrying the identical data.tls.fingerprint observed in step 1. Loopback TLS on a high non-standard port reusing the self-signed cert = listener staging / capability test.", "first_seen": "2026-02-24T02:14:09+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Command and Control — Self-Signed TLS Beacon (CN mismatch)", "technique": "T1587.003", "what_happened": "Between 02:18:02 and 02:23:14 UTC, host 10.108.57.177 made three outbound TLS connections on port 443 to data.tls.sni values corp-mail-eu.com, corp-portal-staging.net and secure-corp-update.app. In each handshake the presented certificate CN did NOT match the SNI / destination hostname (CN remained 'corp-internal-tools'), and the client accepted the certificate anyway despite the mismatch — the classic insecure-client C2 beacon pattern. The three corp-themed lookalike domains are likely attacker-controlled C2 / staging infrastructure.", "chain_reason": "Three Suricata TLS events data.srcip=10.108.57.177 data.dstport=443 with data.tls.sni in {corp-mail-eu.com, corp-portal-staging.net, secure-corp-update.app} where the certificate CN ('corp-internal-tools') does not match data.tls.sni. CN-mismatch accepted by the client across multiple corp-lookalike domains = self-signed-cert C2 beaconing.", "first_seen": "2026-02-24T02:18:02+0000"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Correlation — Capability Development Pattern", "technique": "T1587.003", "what_happened": "Across the 02:11-02:24 UTC window Wazuh correlated 5 Suricata TLS-anomaly alerts from the single internal source 10.108.57.177 sharing one self-signed certificate fingerprint: 1 initial self-signed handshake, 1 loopback listener test, and 3 CN-mismatch outbound beacons to corp-lookalike domains. The short certificate validity (30 days) plus the reuse of one fingerprint across loopback-then-external destinations is the developing/testing-then-deploying signature of MITRE T1587.003 (Develop Capabilities: Digital Certificates).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule grouped 5 Suricata TLS-anomaly events (rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'tls') from the same data.srcip=10.108.57.177 sharing one data.tls.fingerprint within a 15-minute window. The MITRE mapping T1587.003 was attached by the rule because the cert is self-signed, masquerades via CN, and is being staged then deployed.", "first_seen": "2026-02-24T02:24:00+0000"}
  ],
